WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:03.570
-Also, we've had
an especially great week

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.971
because we've had Fred Armisen
back with us

00:00:05.971 --> 00:00:08.741
leading the band on drums.

00:00:08.741 --> 00:00:11.710
And Fred, every time I get
to spend a week with you,

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:13.179
the most delightful part
is hearing

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:16.448
about all your other projects,
the amazing things you're doing.

00:00:16.448 --> 00:00:17.851
You just really are...

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:20.353
You are the multi-est
of multi-hyphenates.

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:21.520
-Thank you.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.124
-And... this is obviously
the time of year

00:00:24.124 --> 00:00:25.725
a lot of people
are going apple picking.

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:28.060
I heard that you just
bought an apple orchard,

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:31.664
but you're not going to use it
in the conventional way.

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:35.301
-No, I'm going to fill it more
with books,

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:39.238
because there's something
very not intellectual

00:00:39.238 --> 00:00:40.873
about apple picking.

00:00:40.873 --> 00:00:45.277
So I thought, well, how can we
make this a more brainy affair?

00:00:45.277 --> 00:00:47.379
How can we do it
so that people discuss books

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:49.416
and talk about literature?

00:00:49.416 --> 00:00:52.385
So, we don't have
the shelves quite yet,

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:53.886
but we've got, like,
piles of books.

00:00:53.886 --> 00:00:57.390
So you go in there, and instead
of apple picking, pick a book.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.557
[ Laughter ]

00:00:58.557 --> 00:00:59.992
-So there are --
let me just clarify --

00:00:59.992 --> 00:01:02.295
there are actual apple trees
with apples,

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:06.399
but you want people to come
and just go get a book instead?

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:08.067
-Yeah. So it's sort
of like, "Okay, great.

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:11.004
These are apples. Fantastic.
Let's talk about these books."

00:01:11.004 --> 00:01:12.505
-So wait, I guess
my question would be,

00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:14.440
it seems like you wanted
to open a bookstore,

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.642
and why wouldn't you
just do that?

00:01:16.642 --> 00:01:18.444
-I thought the same thing.

00:01:18.444 --> 00:01:20.846
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.846 --> 00:01:22.015
-Uh-huh.

00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:23.950
[ Laughter ]

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.851
-Like, why wouldn't I want
to just do that?

00:01:25.851 --> 00:01:29.588
But then, I had the orchard,
and these people showed up,

00:01:29.588 --> 00:01:31.390
and they, you know,
they bought tickets,

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:34.560
and I was like, "Look, let's --
let's talk instead.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:35.962
Let's -- Let's read."

00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:38.031
-Is it against --
Is it against the rules

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:41.768
if they want to get an apple?
-Yes. It's very --

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:43.837
So I want to tell everyone
it's very against the rules

00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:45.705
if you want to get an apple.

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:48.241
-[ Laughs ]
What's it called?

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:50.276
-We don't have a name for it.
-Okay.

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:52.111
Why would you?
I feel like it's --

00:01:52.111 --> 00:01:53.545
I mean, basically,
it sells itself.

00:01:53.545 --> 00:01:55.081
-Thank you.
-You're welcome.

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:57.317
-Thanks.
-Can I come?

00:01:57.317 --> 00:01:59.952
-Please. Yes.
-Can I --

00:01:59.952 --> 00:02:03.223
If I'm very hungry,
hypothetically...

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:06.559
I'm not --

00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:08.394
I'm -- I'm not -- I'm not even
going to ask. [ Laughs ]

